Q: How to select and then delete cell in TStringGrid in Delphi? A: <p>Found an anwser:</p>
<p>Create procedure:</p>
<pre><code>procedure StringGridDeleteRow( AStringGrid:TStringGrid; ARow:integer );
var
  nRow:integer;
begin
  with AStringGrid do
    begin
      for nRow := ARow to RowCount - 2 do
        Rows[nRow].Assign(Rows[nRow+1]);
      Rows[RowCount-1].Clear;
      RowCount := RowCount - 1
    end;
end;
</code></pre>
<p>and then add this line to your button:</p>
<pre><code>StringGridDeleteRow(StringGrid1, StringGrid1.Row);
</code></pre>
<p>It was easier than I thought!</p>

Q: How to scan and remove maclious file when uploading? A: <p>If you want to validate uploads locally without sending data to external APIs (for privacy/compliance), the traditional route is installing <strong>ClamAV</strong>. However, setting up the <code>clamd</code> daemon and managing TCP streams in Node.js can be operationally complex and resource-heavy for many applications.</p>
<p>For a lighter, pure Node.js approach, you can use <strong><a href="https://github.com/pompelmi/pompelmi" rel="nofollow noreferrer">pompelmi</a></strong>. It runs entirely in-process (no external daemons needed) and checks for common malicious patterns like &quot;Zip bombs&quot;, massive recursion, and file masquerading (mismatched magic bytes/extensions).</p>
<p>It saves you from managing a separate antivirus server while still catching common automated attacks and anomalies.</p>
<p>Here is how you can use it to scan a file buffer or stream:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { composeScanners, createZipBombGuard, CommonHeuristicsScanner } from 'pompelmi';

// 1. Configure the scanner (Heuristics + Zip Guard)
const scanner = composeScanners([
  ['zipGuard', createZipBombGuard({ maxEntries: 1000 })],
  ['heuristics', CommonHeuristicsScanner]
]);

// 2. Scan the file buffer
try {
  // Returns an array of matches (empty [] = clean)
  const result = await scanner.scan(fileBuffer);

  if (result.length &gt; 0) {
    console.error(&quot;Malicious file detected:&quot;, result);
    // Block the upload (e.g., return 422)
  } else {
    // File is clean, proceed with saving
  }
} catch (err) {
  console.error(&quot;Scan error:&quot;, err);
}
</code></pre>
<p>It is a good middle ground if you need local security checks without the operational overhead of maintaining a full ClamAV instance.</p>

Q: What is the benefit of another API useEffectEvent over old useRef? A: <p>The comparison isn't correct because these are different ways to achieve the same goal, and <code>refIncrement</code> is relatively ugly workaround for that.</p>
<p>A more closer solution would be:</p>
<pre><code>...
const onTickRef = ref();
onTickRef.current = () =&gt; setCount(c =&gt; c + increment);
...
onTickRef.current();
</code></pre>
<p>This is <a href="https://github.com/sanity-io/use-effect-event/blob/v2.0.3/src/useEffectEvent.ts#L24-L51" rel="nofollow noreferrer">accurate <code>useEventEffect</code> ponyfill</a> that shows that it does roughly the same, but with a guard that prevents calling a function during render and without the need to access <code>current</code> to unwrap function ref.</p>
